\description{
These methods provide the first review surface for the result returned by
\code{\link[=score_mfrm_calibration]{score_mfrm_calibration()}}. \code{print()} gives a compact batch disposition,
\code{summary()} exposes readable score and review tables, and \code{plot()} shows
conditional score uncertainty or numerical review quantities without
refitting a model.

Manage a fail-closed, versioned portable calibration lifecycle (\code{draft} ->
\code{"validated"} -> \code{"frozen"} -> \code{"superseded"} / \code{"retired"}) for scoring new
response rows without training data or an executable fit object. Only a
\code{"frozen"} artifact can be passed to \code{\link[=score_mfrm_calibration]{score_mfrm_calibration()}}.

\code{print()} gives a compact lifecycle and scope overview. \code{summary()} returns
the same identifiers together with coordinate and anchor counts and the
structured validation refusals, if any. Both methods inspect the stored
artifact and do not refit a model or consult training responses.
}
\details{
The default interval plot shows posterior EAP estimates and central
intervals. \code{type = "precision"} plots the number of valid response rows
against posterior SD. \code{type = "edge_mass"} compares posterior mass on the
two outer quadrature nodes with the recorded review threshold. Review
dispositions are highlighted in every view.

These are score-batch review displays, not calibration-fit diagnostics.
Posterior SDs and intervals are conditional on the frozen point calibration
and the actual scoring prior. They exclude calibration-parameter uncertainty.
For GPCM or an explicitly supplied scoring prior, summaries and plot data
retain both original and actual prior values without rounding, the per-Person
integration checks, and (for GPCM) the source decision recorded at extraction.
These records describe conditional scoring, not a new evaluation of the
training fit or the suitability of its population for another cohort.
\code{summary(x)$settings$score_integration_review} compares the reported EAP/SD
with higher-order adaptive references. The separate \code{quadrature_review}
compares adaptive and fixed grids; for an adaptive scorer, that fixed grid
did not produce the reported scores. Printing distinguishes the two checks.
Persons with no valid responses have no score coordinate and are retained
in \code{summary(x)$review} and in the plot payload's
\code{unplotted_dispositions} component.

The summary object retains every returned score and review disposition.
Its estimates table preserves the available estimate and uncertainty basis
columns, calibration identifiers, scoring algorithm and requested interval
level when extracted or written to CSV. The interval level is not rounded.
Older score results recover these fields from their recorded settings when
re-summarized. Saved grid-based intervals retain their original endpoints;
printed results and interval plots explain that their posterior mass can
differ from the requested level.
Its print method shows at most ten rows from each table so routine console
output stays compact. Plots distinguish scored and review states by shape
as well as colour.
\code{plot(x)} returns a \code{ggplot} object directly; \code{as_ggplot(p)} and
\code{as_ggplot(p, component = "data")} preserve the same selected view, saved
bounds, review symbols and unplotted dispositions for both \code{ggplot} and
legacy \code{mfrm_plot_data} objects.
Use \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} to extract other components. Changing the interval level
requires an explicit scoring call; conversion does not recompute intervals.
Notes remain in the returned object and are not automatically drawn.

\strong{Supported formats and source checks.}
\itemize{
\item \strong{Format 1}: ordinary RSM/PCM MML (\code{N(0, 1)} prior, retaining anchors and
two-way interactions; requires \code{quadrature_review} from
\code{\link[=mml_quadrature_sensitivity]{mml_quadrature_sensitivity()}} with \code{fit} equal to the highest-grid fit).
\item \strong{Format 2}: one-family GPCM MML (unit weights, no anchors/interactions,
intercept-only normal population; checks local likelihood, gradient,
unregularized Hessian, and source/batch integration).
\item \strong{Format 3 / Format 4}: unit-weight unanchored RSM/PCM JML (format 3) and
shared-owner GPCM JML (format 4, checking positive-definite joint curvature
with reciprocal condition \verb{> 1e-10}), scored under a post-hoc \code{N(0, 1)}
reference prior.
\item \strong{Format 5}: experimental corrected GPCM JML (\code{jml_correction_order}),
checking mean-equation residual \verb{<= 1e-7} and Newton step \verb{<= 1e-5}.
\item \strong{Formats 6 & 7}: experimental two-family GPCM MML (\code{N(0, 1)} prior, two
slope owners, plus extended named \code{slope_reference}/\code{noncenter_facet} roles
in format 7; \code{row_dispositions$ObservedContext} flags novel facet combinations).
}

New v2 artifacts invert the continuous posterior CDF for equal-tail intervals
while retaining stored quadrature for EAP and \code{SD} (conditional on the frozen
point calibration and prior, excluding calibration/anchor uncertainty;
Bock & Mislevy, 1982, \doi{10.1177/014662168200600405}; Muraki, 1992,
\doi{10.1177/014662169201600206}). See
\code{mfrmr_output_guide("calibration_capabilities")}.

A calibration artifact intentionally has no \code{plot()} method. Its coordinate
table mixes facet, step, and interaction parameter roles, and the artifact
does not contain calibration-parameter uncertainty. Plot returned score
batches with \link{mfrm_calibration_score_methods} instead; inspect
\code{calibration$parameters$coordinates} when auditing stored point coordinates.
}
